    "created_at": "2019-10-11T21:37:08Z",
    "updated_at": "2019-10-11T21:37:08Z",
    "body": "The power and purpose of inputMode as I see it is that it governs the layout of the virtual keyboard.  For example, URL/email/number and others all have a specialized virtual keyboard user interfaces on Windows and other operating systems as well.\r\n\r\nI think because the user can manually show the virtual keyboard using OS UI, the inputMode should be set correctly all the time.  That way when the virtual keyboard appears (which may happen even when inputMode=none if the user touches the virtual keyboard button in Windows), it has the correct layout.\r\n\r\nHere's a scenario to help us think through the implications of various API shapes:  \r\n\r\n1. Microsoft Excel wants the virtual keyboard to appear automatically when the formula bar is focused.\r\n2. They want it to not appear on the first tap when a cell is focused (because the first tap should just select the cell).\r\n3. If the user taps a second time on the selected cell, the virtual keyboard should appear and the cell transitions into an edit mode, e.g. revealing a formula backing the cell that can now be edited in place.\r\n4. Additionally, if the user has a hardware keyboard, or manually reveals a virtual keyboard, and the user starts typing while a cell is in the selected state, it should immediately enter an edit mode where the contents of the cell are replaced with whatever the user types.\r\n\r\nMy take away from these scenarios is that if Excel implements these behaviors by initially having inputMode=none, and the user manually reveals the virtual keyboard (so they can perform scenario step 4), it will have a generic shape.  The generic shape might be appropriate for Excel, but hopefully the point is not lost.  If Excel wanted a date-shaped keyboard for date formatted cells it would be a problem.\r\n\r\nAs an alternative, I'm thinking we should use a new attribute like inputPanelPolicy on the element.  It has values like \"automatic\", \"manual\", and maybe \"tap-to-reveal\".  This attribute works as described above by @domenic / @mustaqahmed to control whether the virtual keyboard *may* be displayed or hidden by the OS when the element is focused.\r\n\r\nThoughts?\r\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
